WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.870
- I talk about the Larry
Elder interview in that

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.920
and where, after he beat me senseless,

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:08.070
my editors and my
director and my producers

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:08.903
wanted to cut it.

00:00:08.903 --> 00:00:10.187
They didn't want it to air and I said,

00:00:10.187 --> 00:00:13.257
"Guys, if I'm anything
that I purport to be

00:00:13.257 --> 00:00:15.100
"as an interviewer, we have to put it up."

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.060
And I saw it get clipped
a million times on YouTube

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:21.513
and "Larry Elder Destroys" in
capital letters, "Libtard",

00:00:21.513 --> 00:00:23.270
and you know, and blah blah blah.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:25.730
But what I realized
almost within a day was,

00:00:25.730 --> 00:00:28.990
holy cow, people were
having their wake-up moment

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.527
right alongside of me.

00:00:30.527 --> 00:00:33.777
(rhythmic synth music)

00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:40.710
- Hey, everyone, it's Andrew Klavan.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:43.380
We're here with the Don't
Burn This Book book club,

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.300
we're doing chapter five,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.310
Check Your Facts, Not Your Privilege.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.260
Which is a chapter after my own heart,

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:50.450
I'm very big on facts.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:53.500
It reminded me of what
Ronald Reagan once said.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.449
He said, "It's not that
Democrats don't know anything,

00:00:56.449 --> 00:00:59.250
"it's that everything they
know happens to be untrue."

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:01.240
And I think that this
is something that comes

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.590
to all of us when we are,
as they say, red pilled.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.350
When we start to realize
that all the things

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:07.570
we took for granted,

00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:09.070
all the positions we took for granted,

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.623
really aren't based in any kind of facts.

00:01:10.623 --> 00:01:14.044
They're based in a kind
of emotional brow beating

00:01:14.044 --> 00:01:17.527
that the Left uses instead of ideas.

00:01:17.527 --> 00:01:19.240
And this is one of the
things about the Left,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.390
they don't have a lot of ideas

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:21.730
but they have a lot of techniques

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.680
for silencing people with ideas

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:25.770
and for frightening people with ideas.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:28.090
And I have to say, one of
the things I liked about,

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.790
this chapter's packed with facts,

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:31.800
it's packed with information,
stuff you can use

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.820
to beat up your
grandmother at Thanksgiving

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.450
if she has a bad opinion.
(Dave laughing)

00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:38.320
But I think the most
important part of this chapter

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.470
is the discussion about the fact that

00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:41.870
when we talk to each other,

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.180
when Liberals and Conservatives
talk to one another,

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.540
they are talking in different languages.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.450
I would go even further
in saying they're talking

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.750
from a completely different point of view.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.240
So, Dave, I'd like to
talk to you about that

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.070
'cause that was the thing
that really grabbed me

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.720
was this idea that we're
not communicating at all.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.160
We think we're arguing with one another

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.930
but we're actually not putting
across our ideas at all.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.920
Is that a fair way to say it?

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.960
- It is a fair way and Klaven,
before I say anything else,

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:08.320
one of the reasons that I wanted
you to handle this chapter

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.560
is because I think you
are one of the better

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.610
communicators we have
on these type of ideas

00:02:13.610 --> 00:02:17.790
because often it is that lens
that is the screwed up part.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:20.790
It's hard to tell how people
think about the world,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.490
where their biases are
and everything else.

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:23.323
And I find you to be

00:02:23.323 --> 00:02:24.860
a very clear communicator on this stuff.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.040
And of course you do work
with that fast-talking

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:28.440
orthodox Jew who's always talking,

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.420
what's that thing about
facts and feelings?

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:31.253
Right?

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:32.086
There's something that he's
always saying about that.

00:02:32.086 --> 00:02:32.919
- Yeah.

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:33.752
- So I thought there was--
- Absolutely.

00:02:33.752 --> 00:02:36.510
- You're around him a lot
so I thought it would work.

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:38.530
So, yeah, we can dive into
some of the nitty-gritty

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:42.800
of the specifics but I agree
that the part about the lenses

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:45.670
really is the most interesting part here.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.040
You know, some of us view the
world, so, me, for example,

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:52.510
I really view the world through
mostly what is thought of,

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:55.010
in an American, sense
as a Libertarian lens.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.058
That I want you as the
individual to basically

00:02:59.058 --> 00:03:01.460
be able to do whatever you want

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:03.410
but not purely whatever you want.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.770
You can't infringe on
your neighbor's rights,

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:08.150
you can't steal from people,
you can't murder people,

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:10.420
things like that and this
is where I always say,

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.927
people always say, "Well,
Dave, what's the difference

00:03:11.927 --> 00:03:14.320
"between a Classical
Liberal and a Libertarian?"

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.417
And I always say, "Well,
I want a little bit more

00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:20.467
"of the basic guidelines around society

00:03:20.467 --> 00:03:22.680
"than I think a pure Libertarian does."

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.540
And then we have people that view things

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.570
through more of a Conservative lens

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.570
that generally is more that
they're trying to defend

00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:32.130
the family and sort of long-fought

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:38.510
battles that they view
built Western civilization

00:03:38.510 --> 00:03:41.320
that has sort of fought against barbarism

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.180
as they would call it.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.360
And I actually have
become very sympathetic

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:45.740
to that view as well.

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.842
And then there's sort
of the more progressive,

00:03:47.842 --> 00:03:50.290
or, I think it's really
called Liberal lens,

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:52.630
although I always am trying
to clean up the difference

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:54.420
between sort of Progressive and Liberal.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:58.210
Which is more sort of about
the oppressed and the oppressor

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:00.920
and I think that's the most
muddled lens these days

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.466
because if you live in the West,

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:06.000
and especially if you
live in the United States,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:08.190
you are not oppressed, you have actually

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:10.140
the greatest privilege there is.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.000
Which is that you live in a free society.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.030
That isn't to say it is perfect.

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:16.020
We know it is not perfect.

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:18.700
But I don't believe a
perfect society can exist.

00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:22.560
I think once you try to
create a perfect society,

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.810
you're gonna have to take
out a lot of people to do it

00:04:24.810 --> 00:04:26.630
because people are imperfect.

00:04:26.630 --> 00:04:30.725
So I think that general
idea of sort of figuring out

00:04:30.725 --> 00:04:34.250
how you look at the world,
what sort of pecking order

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.620
you put the importance of these things in,

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.480
is a really important thing
and then, to your point,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.170
then when you find oh,
well, here's someone

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.640
who looks at the world
so differently than me,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.340
they don't accept facts as I accept facts.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.820
Or they have what they view to be

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.440
a completely different set of facts.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:51.910
Well, then you can start
unpacking that a little bit

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.880
because you can have an appreciation,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:57.180
oh, it's just that their
hierarchy is a little different

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:58.920
than my hierarchy and
we have to figure out

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.200
how to be able to do some talking here.

00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:04.770
- You know, like you, I
came over from the Left,

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.020
and I shifted over to the
Right almost not knowing it

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:08.843
'cause I was out of the
country when it happened.

00:05:08.843 --> 00:05:11.204
I was living in England and I came back

00:05:11.204 --> 00:05:13.410
and found I had shifted to the right.

00:05:13.410 --> 00:05:16.350
And I had this innocent
sense that oh, wow, you know,

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.580
if only my fellow friends on the Left knew

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:22.780
what I now knew, they would
see that they have made

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.310
this terrible mistake and
I went around explaining

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.030
to my former friends who
now no longer talk to me.

00:05:28.030 --> 00:05:31.210
And when I follow you on
Twitter, I see you often

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:35.210
saying to people, "What you
just said is not based in fact"

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:37.397
or "Now you're starting to see the facts.

00:05:37.397 --> 00:05:40.080
"Why don't you come on my
show and we'll talk about it."

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.050
How often do people respond to that?

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.340
How often do you get
people who are willing

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:47.070
to engage with you in this journey?

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:50.000
- Well, unfortunately,
it's becoming less and less

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:54.640
because so many of the people on the Left,

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:59.250
I think, have turned
from, say, honest debate

00:05:59.250 --> 00:06:01.550
to just personal attacks.

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.150
And I am always willing to open my door,

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.200
or in this case my Skype,

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.580
to anyone that wants to
talk to me respectfully.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.330
But if you're endlessly going to attack me

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.240
or attack my motives or
lie about me, you know,

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:14.210
there's a certain set of people

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:16.267
that will just lie about you
relentlessly and then say,

00:06:16.267 --> 00:06:18.150
"See, and you won't
bring me on your show."

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:20.210
And it's like, well,
that's not really the game

00:06:20.210 --> 00:06:22.840
that I'm interested in
playing but it is fascinating

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.700
to me what you're talking
about there which is

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:27.560
that you thought, 'cause I
think a lot of ex-Lefties,

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.190
which, by the way, I think
is truly the biggest-growing

00:06:31.190 --> 00:06:33.881
and unspoken political
group in the United States.

00:06:33.881 --> 00:06:38.430
It's the decent Liberals who
are waking up against Leftism.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:40.370
But I think a lot of
them are sort of thinking

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:42.187
what you just laid out there which is,

00:06:42.187 --> 00:06:44.037
"Oh, I've woken up to some stuff,

00:06:44.037 --> 00:06:48.927
"it clearly is more fact
based and more freeing

00:06:48.927 --> 00:06:51.307
"as a human being and
suddenly I'm realizing,

00:06:51.307 --> 00:06:53.517
"oh, these Conservatives that
I thought were the bad guys,

00:06:53.517 --> 00:06:55.409
"or whatever, they're not so bad."

00:06:55.409 --> 00:06:59.140
But they're afraid to say it
and not only afraid to say it,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.420
they're shocked that people
don't start coming with them.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:05.760
But there's a reason for that
and the reason, of course,

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:07.730
is that once you've called everybody

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:08.730
else bigots and racists,

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.920
you know that will be turned against you.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:12.950
But one of the things to
just further your point,

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.270
you know, it's interesting
'cause I know you and I have

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:19.000
still some pretty significant
political disagreements.

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.460
You are pro-life, I make a
pro-choice argument in this book.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.019
I think you're for the
death penalty, right?

00:07:25.019 --> 00:07:27.510
- I'm not for the death
penalty as it stands

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:29.480
but I'm potentially for the death penalty.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:31.330
- Okay, so, I'm against the death penalty,

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.800
I've heard some great
arguments, Dennis Prager makes,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.050
I think, a fantastic argument
for the death penalty.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.120
I just don't like the idea

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.640
of giving the state that much power.

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:42.520
I'm still for some level
of public education,

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:44.230
I'm for dignity with
death, you should be able

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.440
to make choices at the end.

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:46.910
I'm for legalizing marijuana, I mean,

00:07:46.910 --> 00:07:47.990
we could go into a bunch of things

00:07:47.990 --> 00:07:50.040
that you and I don't
really see eye to eye on.

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:51.700
But I think the purpose of this chapter

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:54.740
was also to show people,
people on the right,

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.188
they're willing to sit there and do it.

00:07:57.188 --> 00:08:01.630
Sit there and debate it and go through

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.770
all the grit and the dirt
that you have to get to

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:07.940
to be able to form a functioning society.

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:11.160
And unfortunately, that
doesn't really exist

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:12.790
on the other side.

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:15.730
- You know, you start this
chapter, this is chapter five,

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:17.290
Check Your Facts, Not Your Privilege,

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:20.300
you start it with you having
a debate with Larry Elder

00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:22.300
and I did feel sorry for
you 'cause that's like

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.780
shooting a nail gun into
your foot, you know?

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:27.989
I mean, the guy has a real
mastery of facts and information.

00:08:27.989 --> 00:08:32.989
But it was about racism
and black people in America

00:08:33.530 --> 00:08:36.040
and we're having this
conversation on the day

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:37.540
when the Pulitzer Prize is being given

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:40.490
to The New York Times for
a completely dishonest take

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:44.163
on slavery and black
people, their 1619 Project.

00:08:44.163 --> 00:08:48.880
So much of the division between
us is keyed around race.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.260
A lot of it is keyed around
gender but so much of it starts

00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:56.070
with this idea that
somehow we are perpetually

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.440
in a racist state of systemic
racism in this country.

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.260
And Larry Elder, in your
chapter, he basically takes you

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:04.093
to task and he says,

00:09:04.093 --> 00:09:06.318
"Show me, show me where that racism is."

00:09:06.318 --> 00:09:09.817
The question I get asked
more often than any other is,

00:09:09.817 --> 00:09:14.037
"How can I convince people
that we are not in this state?

00:09:14.037 --> 00:09:18.567
"How can I convince people
that we can actually become

00:09:18.567 --> 00:09:22.070
"one country despite
the color differences?"

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:24.230
Is that something that
you feel is possible

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.160
or are these two different languages

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.160
just so completely separate that we can't

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.990
reach across that barrier?

00:09:29.990 --> 00:09:31.620
- You know, it's such a great question

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.440
and I would say this, I don't
think I could do what I do

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.340
and I suspect you couldn't do what you do

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.410
if you didn't think it was
possible to bridge this divide.

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:40.320
It would be like,

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.490
what would we really be
doing with our lives?

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.070
Why would we be talking about these things

00:09:44.070 --> 00:09:46.410
if we didn't think that the good ideas

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.080
could ultimately conquer the bad ones?

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.031
Or that the sunlight
would expose the bad ideas

00:09:52.031 --> 00:09:53.020
or the rest of it?

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:56.500
So, you know, I talk about the
Larry Elder interview in that

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.540
and where, after he beat me senseless,

00:09:59.540 --> 00:10:01.700
my editors and my
director and my producers

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:02.533
wanted to cut it.

00:10:02.533 --> 00:10:03.827
They didn't want it to air and I said,

00:10:03.827 --> 00:10:06.887
"Guys, if I'm anything
that I purport to be

00:10:06.887 --> 00:10:08.710
"as an interviewer, we have to put it up."

00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.670
And I saw it get clipped
a million times on YouTube

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:15.480
and "Larry Elder destroys,"
in capital letters, "libtard."

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:16.890
And you know blah, blah, blah.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:19.370
But what I realized
almost within a day was

00:10:19.370 --> 00:10:22.610
holy cow, people were
having their wake-up moment

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:23.880
right alongside of me.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.950
So as I write, I don't know
how many people in our biz,

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:30.350
or in any business, can
say my best and worst

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:33.090
professional moment were
at the exact same time.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:34.350
But that's truly what happened.

00:10:34.350 --> 00:10:37.700
It was my worst moment
because I came to a gunfight

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:40.557
not ready, with no weapon
attached, because I just thought,

00:10:40.557 --> 00:10:42.567
"Oh, I say things" and
that's what Lefties do,

00:10:42.567 --> 00:10:45.210
that's it sort of, just because
you say it, it is right.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.700
Systemic racism exists, it is right.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:51.161
America is racist, it's
divinely right in and of itself.

00:10:51.161 --> 00:10:53.090
So it was my worst moment because of that

00:10:53.090 --> 00:10:55.300
but it was my best
because in the face of it,

00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:58.850
once I got countered, I
don't know where it came from

00:10:58.850 --> 00:11:00.580
exactly but I showed some humility

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:03.410
and I let it be a learning
experience for other people.

00:11:03.410 --> 00:11:05.360
But to directly answer your question about

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:06.743
can we get through this,

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.490
I think there's a lot of
people that in many ways

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:11.870
don't want us to get through this.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:14.433
There's a lot of people
that thrive on keeping

00:11:14.433 --> 00:11:19.010
racial tensions stoked
and it's the anti-racists

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:22.640
who actually are pushing the
most racism into society,

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.430
the supposed anti-racists,
who are the ones that are

00:11:25.430 --> 00:11:29.320
obsessed with our color of
our skin and our sexuality

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:31.020
and our gender and the rest of it.

00:11:31.020 --> 00:11:33.220
And then it's the other
side that just doesn't care

00:11:33.220 --> 00:11:35.407
about that stuff and then they use it as,

00:11:35.407 --> 00:11:38.017
"Oh, you don't care what
color someone's skin is?

00:11:38.017 --> 00:11:38.990
"You're the racist."

00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:40.710
And it's like, no,
that's actually reverse,

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:42.410
we gotta work through this thing.

00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:44.100
Can we get through it, I think, is like,

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:45.850
that's the billion-dollar question.

00:11:45.850 --> 00:11:50.357
And I think the answer, if
we can wake up enough people,

00:11:50.357 --> 00:11:52.750
and that's exactly why I wrote the book.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.720
If we can wake up enough people

00:11:54.720 --> 00:11:58.040
to exactly what individual rights are,

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.470
to why the Constitution is so great,

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:03.760
to why America, unlike any other country

00:12:03.760 --> 00:12:04.593
in the history of the world,

00:12:04.593 --> 00:12:07.840
that not only brought all of
these people here to blend in

00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:10.170
but also to keep their
traditions and ethnicity

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.250
and all of those things.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:13.920
But we also expand
rights over time, right?

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.650
We had slaves, we got rid of it.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:17.130
Then black people could vote.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:19.238
Women couldn't vote, now they can vote.

00:12:19.238 --> 00:12:20.700
Gay people can get married.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.480
We've expanded and expanded
and expanded rights

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:26.770
and I know that it will
be my Conservative friends

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:30.190
who, if in the future, there's
some other group of people

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:33.040
who don't have equality,
equality under the law,

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:34.750
it doesn't mean you have
to morally like them

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:35.640
or something like that.

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:38.180
I know that it will be
the liberty-minded people

00:12:38.180 --> 00:12:41.570
that will defend people's ability
to be equal under the law.

00:12:41.570 --> 00:12:44.560
While it's the Lefties
who will be sort of using

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:46.510
them as a crudgel to gain power.

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:48.826
And that really is the
important distinction.

00:12:48.826 --> 00:12:50.690
You're not just anti-racist

00:12:50.690 --> 00:12:53.140
because you say you're anti-racist.

00:12:53.140 --> 00:12:56.350
You're anti-racist when you
get out of everyone's lives

00:12:56.350 --> 00:12:59.780
so that they can live
whatever their best life is.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:03.870
- You now find yourself in what
I think is the biggest tent,

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:06.820
which is the Conservative
side or the right-wing side,

00:13:06.820 --> 00:13:08.150
whatever you wanna call it,

00:13:08.150 --> 00:13:11.030
where there are in fact
people, as you said yourself,

00:13:11.030 --> 00:13:13.070
there are people who
hold your points of view

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.161
but there are people who hold
very different points of view.

00:13:16.161 --> 00:13:18.720
And yet, and yet, there is something

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.430
that is bringing us all together.

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.740
There is something that makes it easy

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:25.153
for Ben Shapiro and
Dave Rubin to be friends

00:13:25.153 --> 00:13:28.820
even when they have complete
differences of opinions.

00:13:28.820 --> 00:13:31.850
What is the moment, you know,
you talked about gay marriage

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:33.530
and more freedom for gay
people, I'm old enough,

00:13:33.530 --> 00:13:36.605
I remember people being
literally arrested for being gay.

00:13:36.605 --> 00:13:39.660
The police could go into a gay bar

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:41.340
and wait 'til somebody hit on 'em,

00:13:41.340 --> 00:13:44.560
and arrest you for that so I
actually remember that time

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.280
and it's a huge, huge, change.

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:48.760
And I personally have been glad to see it

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.240
but for awhile on the Right,
I was talking to myself.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.990
For a while I was saying, you
know, we're wrong about this,

00:13:52.990 --> 00:13:55.750
we should shift over and
it has shifted somewhat.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:59.570
But you're still in this tent with people

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:01.670
who strongly disagree with you.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.320
What is it, do you
think, that unites them?

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:07.070
That unites you to people that you know

00:14:07.070 --> 00:14:08.580
might have feelings about you which

00:14:08.580 --> 00:14:12.070
are not as open as you'd like them to be?

00:14:12.070 --> 00:14:16.540
- I think the literal
answer is individual rights,

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:18.720
that that is the bedrock
principle of the Right,

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:19.630
individual rights.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:23.782
If you are a member of a society,
a legal member of society,

00:14:23.782 --> 00:14:26.630
then you should be treated
equally under the law.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:29.450
We are not gonna re-jigger
and re-organize things

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:31.790
so that everyone is exactly equal

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.980
and I've said this many
times but some people

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:36.390
are born into more money, some
people are born into less.

00:14:36.390 --> 00:14:38.330
Some people are born drug addicted,

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:41.320
some people are genuinely luckier in life.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:42.480
Some people make their luck.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:44.132
Some people work hard, some people don't.

00:14:44.132 --> 00:14:46.770
The Lefties are all
obsessed with those things.

00:14:46.770 --> 00:14:50.060
Society can't fix those things,
that's actually impossible.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:52.330
That's what the quest of life is about.

00:14:52.330 --> 00:14:54.090
Society can just make it equal.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:56.680
So I think that's the literal answer.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:15:00.783
I think the figurative answer is sort of,

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:05.220
from an American perspective,
I think almost everyone

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:08.170
that identifies as a
Conservative or a Libertarian

00:15:08.170 --> 00:15:11.580
or a Classical Liberal or
let's say a disaffected Lefty,

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:13.960
I think there's an
innate pride in America.

00:15:13.960 --> 00:15:17.790
I am deeply, deeply proud,
it's not even proud.

00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:20.530
Truly, I thank God that I am an American,

00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:21.960
that I was born in this country

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:23.660
that is the freest place ever.

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:25.500
You've been to my house and my studio,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:28.400
I have a giant American
flag sitting in there,

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:29.870
it's about five foot by eight foot.

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:31.660
We've got the Constitution
and the Bill of Rights

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:32.870
on the wall in there, too.

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:34.750
I'm not doing that for theater.

00:15:34.750 --> 00:15:36.720
These are the greatest man-made documents

00:15:36.720 --> 00:15:37.570
that have freed us.

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:39.680
And I think there is a pride in that

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.420
that is deeply special on the Right.

00:15:41.420 --> 00:15:43.750
And now try to think about
what's happening on the Left.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:47.470
I mean, go through the
candidates that ran.

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:50.280
The election and the primary
seem like a lifetime ago.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:52.820
But it was like, how often
were the words liberty

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:57.390
or freedom or Constitution
ever said, ever said?

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:59.500
I keep telling people that
the one time Constitution

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:01.920
was said was when Kamala Harris said

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:03.600
she would do an executive action on guns

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:05.530
and Biden was like, "Hey, but
what about the Constitution?"

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:07.170
And she literally laughed in his face.

00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.520
Is Bernie Sanders proud of
the American experiment,

00:16:11.520 --> 00:16:13.700
the thing that he says is racist

00:16:13.700 --> 00:16:17.580
and he basically talks about
as some sort of evil thing?

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:22.510
And that thing, it's dangerous,
it's actually dangerous.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:25.150
It doesn't mean that America
is perfect or always right

00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:27.970
or doesn't make bad
decisions or one president

00:16:27.970 --> 00:16:29.950
is better than the other
or something like that.

00:16:29.950 --> 00:16:34.320
But I think the ethos of let's
all be in on this together

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.980
and it doesn't matter if
your great-grandparents

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:41.640
came from Poland and my
great-grandparents came from Korea

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:43.990
or any other thing, it's
like, if you came here

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:46.880
and you work hard, that
ethos is strong on the right.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:50.360
I'm not sure what the defining
thing on the left is anymore.

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:53.730
- When you go through your
list of facts in this chapter,

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:58.490
you have a hilarious
line about the pay gap

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:01.100
between men and women,
saying that the two things

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:03.010
that would survive an atomic war would be

00:17:03.010 --> 00:17:05.820
cockroaches and this idea
that there's a pay gap

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:06.670
when there's not.

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:10.210
If there was one fact,

00:17:10.210 --> 00:17:12.950
if there was just one piece of information

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:16.230
that you could stamp onto
the forehead of a left-winger

00:17:16.230 --> 00:17:17.797
that might make him
say, "Oh, you know what?

00:17:17.797 --> 00:17:19.220
"I gotta rethink this."

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:20.340
What would it be?

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:23.477
What was the start for
you that made you think,

00:17:23.477 --> 00:17:27.060
"Hey, wait a minute, I've
really got this wrong"?

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.683
What's the string that
pulls the suit apart?

00:17:30.570 --> 00:17:32.800
- You know, I think that the string for me

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:34.830
was that the rubber
started meeting the road.

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:37.240
It wasn't just that I was
waking up to these ideas

00:17:37.240 --> 00:17:38.790
and talking to interesting thinkers.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:40.940
So like when I talked to Thomas Sowell,

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:42.180
and we talked about economics,

00:17:42.180 --> 00:17:46.250
here's a guy who has been
railing sort of against Leftism

00:17:46.250 --> 00:17:48.440
and Communism and Socialism
for his whole life,

00:17:48.440 --> 00:17:51.170
although he was a Marxist
at one time in his life,

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:52.530
and we talk about that, too.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:55.100
So, this is another guy
who was a Lefty like us

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:56.940
and then who evolved into something else.

00:17:56.940 --> 00:17:59.910
But I think what happened
was, as I started talking

00:17:59.910 --> 00:18:02.350
about these things more
and bringing in more people

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:04.690
with a diverse set of
views, at the same time,

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:06.590
I was also building a business here

00:18:06.590 --> 00:18:09.810
and then when I would
hear about why low taxes

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:13.130
and getting rid of regulation
and these things are important

00:18:13.130 --> 00:18:14.770
and then I'd start looking at the numbers

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:16.880
and talking to my business
manager and my accountant

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.290
and everything else and
realizing, man, we live,

00:18:19.290 --> 00:18:20.810
I mean, Andrew, you and I live in a state

00:18:20.810 --> 00:18:23.300
that is obscenely high tax.

00:18:23.300 --> 00:18:25.620
Just in the last week, because of corona,

00:18:25.620 --> 00:18:27.520
I've been sort of half-joking about it

00:18:27.520 --> 00:18:29.240
but it's becoming less of a joke,

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:32.080
thinking about maybe it is
time to get out of California.

00:18:32.080 --> 00:18:34.630
And we were crunching some
numbers and it's like,

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:37.190
the amount that I would
save in a place like

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:40.450
Florida or Texas or Washington
or South Dakota, I mean,

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:43.230
the places that have
basically zero income tax,

00:18:43.230 --> 00:18:45.140
and it's not because I want a yacht.

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:46.240
Although I would be perfectly

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:48.110
within my right to have a yacht.

00:18:48.110 --> 00:18:49.060
It's not that I want a yacht.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:50.960
We only have one car
between the two of us.

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:52.270
It's not that I want more stuff

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:55.060
but I know if my company is taxed less,

00:18:55.060 --> 00:18:58.920
if you get regulation out
of the way, what can I do?

00:18:58.920 --> 00:18:59.753
You know what I would do?

00:18:59.753 --> 00:19:01.040
I would hire more people.

00:19:01.040 --> 00:19:03.030
We just hired a new associate producer

00:19:03.030 --> 00:19:05.190
in the middle of coronavirus
because our business

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:08.890
is doing well and if
you allow the government

00:19:08.890 --> 00:19:12.180
to get out of the way and allow
you to keep what you earn,

00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:14.790
I'm pretty sure that
everyone watching this

00:19:14.790 --> 00:19:16.630
knows what to do with their money

00:19:16.630 --> 00:19:18.075
better than the government does.

00:19:18.075 --> 00:19:21.100
So, I would say the one thing is,

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:23.670
when you get to that moment,
because it will happen.

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.020
You know, we can talk about all this stuff

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:27.510
at the idea level, low
taxes, states' rights,

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:28.790
all of these things.

00:19:28.790 --> 00:19:32.160
But if you live your life
sort of congruently with that,

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:34.820
if you live your life where
you're trying to apply

00:19:34.820 --> 00:19:36.010
your ideals into your life,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:37.980
at some point it will click.

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:39.980
And then suddenly there
will be a real moment

00:19:39.980 --> 00:19:41.417
right in front of you
where you'll be like,

00:19:41.417 --> 00:19:44.126
"Holy cow, the stuff that
I've been talking about,

00:19:44.126 --> 00:19:46.647
"here are the papers,
here are the numbers.

00:19:46.647 --> 00:19:50.217
"And it's in front of me and now I know

00:19:50.217 --> 00:19:52.980
"that I'm not just saying
it, I'm living it."

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:54.410
- I gotta ask you one last question

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:55.810
because I have to know this.

00:19:55.810 --> 00:19:58.310
The first time I was on your
show was right after, I think,

00:19:58.310 --> 00:19:59.820
Donald Trump was elected and you asked me

00:19:59.820 --> 00:20:02.390
about Donald Trump and
I told you that I had

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.113
this fear that there was a 5% chance

00:20:04.113 --> 00:20:05.740
that he might be Adolph Hitler.

00:20:05.740 --> 00:20:08.130
But in that other 95% chance,

00:20:08.130 --> 00:20:10.290
he was much better than Hillary Clinton.

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.720
To me, he's turned out to
be a really good president.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:14.250
He's done a very good job.

00:20:14.250 --> 00:20:16.100
He reminds me of the kind of boss you have

00:20:16.100 --> 00:20:17.650
who you sometimes don't like

00:20:17.650 --> 00:20:19.070
but he knows how to run the business.

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:20.770
You know, and I think that that's

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:22.068
a kind of interesting thing.

00:20:22.068 --> 00:20:25.410
Politically, you've
changed philosophically.

00:20:25.410 --> 00:20:27.600
I've watched you change philosophically.

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:28.710
Have you changed politically?

00:20:28.710 --> 00:20:29.967
Is there a point where you start to say,

00:20:29.967 --> 00:20:30.800
"You know what?

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.387
"I'm gonna put my vote
where my philosophy is

00:20:34.387 --> 00:20:36.727
"even if it means voting
for someone like Trump

00:20:36.727 --> 00:20:38.180
"that I don't like that much."

00:20:38.180 --> 00:20:40.240
- Yeah, oh man, this is the big finish

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:41.837
on this one, Klaven, good question.

00:20:41.837 --> 00:20:42.670
(Klaven laughing)

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.190
So first off, let me give
you credit on something else

00:20:44.190 --> 00:20:45.490
because I was also with you,

00:20:45.490 --> 00:20:48.390
I was with the Daily Wire
guys the night of the election

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:51.650
and you said something that I
have quoted it a million times

00:20:51.650 --> 00:20:53.500
because I think it was so on-point.

00:20:53.500 --> 00:20:55.740
Everyone was shocked
about what was happening

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:59.317
and you said, "You know, what
a beautiful thing in America

00:20:59.317 --> 00:21:02.557
"that it shows you the
flexibility of our system

00:21:02.557 --> 00:21:05.737
"that the thing that everyone
said couldn't happen,

00:21:05.737 --> 00:21:09.017
"that every pollster, every
expert, everyone on TV,

00:21:09.017 --> 00:21:11.707
"every pundit, that the
thing that they just said

00:21:11.707 --> 00:21:13.477
"couldn't happen just happened.

00:21:13.477 --> 00:21:15.707
"What an incredible society we live in

00:21:15.707 --> 00:21:17.397
"that that sort of thing could happen."

00:21:17.397 --> 00:21:18.410
- Agreement is so much more fun, yeah.

00:21:18.410 --> 00:21:21.530
- How much more fun and all of that stuff.

00:21:21.530 --> 00:21:24.150
And you weren't even commenting
on Trump specifically.

00:21:24.150 --> 00:21:25.060
You were just saying like,

00:21:25.060 --> 00:21:27.637
the reality of what just
happened is so beautiful.

00:21:27.637 --> 00:21:29.530
To answer your question,
I will tell you this,

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:31.810
so I am a registered
Democrat in this state

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:34.760
because there is so much
Democrat power in this state

00:21:34.760 --> 00:21:37.030
and I wanted to be able
to vote in the primaries.

00:21:37.030 --> 00:21:39.550
So I voted for Joe Biden
(coughs) excuse me,

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.060
in the primaries but it was really

00:21:41.060 --> 00:21:42.750
just a vote against Bernie.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:46.310
That was what I viewed as this
truly dangerous set of ideas.

00:21:46.310 --> 00:21:49.560
Biden, it was sort of like,
eh, just take the vote.

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.210
You know, something like that.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:52.323
But I will tell you this,

00:21:52.323 --> 00:21:55.600
I voted against every
single measure in there

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:57.540
that increased taxes, every single thing.

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.030
And you have to read them and
they're completely confusing

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:01.810
and they say things like, you know,

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:04.170
"500 million more dollars for education"

00:22:04.170 --> 00:22:05.657
and if you just look at
the headline, you're like,

00:22:05.657 --> 00:22:07.600
"Well, of course I want more education."

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:09.200
And then you have to read,
read, read, read, read,

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:10.033
and on the bottom it says,

00:22:10.033 --> 00:22:12.507
"And this will be intact for nine years

00:22:12.507 --> 00:22:15.340
"and then only can be repealed by vote."

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.820
But no one ever votes
to take education away.

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:20.110
You just always vote to
put more money into it.

00:22:20.110 --> 00:22:24.240
So, I would say, I would say
in effect I have no party.

00:22:24.240 --> 00:22:27.060
I don't think having a party
is actually that important.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.980
There's a functional
utility to having a party

00:22:29.980 --> 00:22:33.100
in states where you can only
vote in a primary by one party.

00:22:33.100 --> 00:22:34.810
But I don't consider myself a Democrat,

00:22:34.810 --> 00:22:36.730
I don't consider myself a Republican.

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:39.690
Do I have far more in
common with Republicans?

00:22:39.690 --> 00:22:40.870
Obviously.

00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:42.250
And to directly answer your question,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:44.890
I would say, at this
point, I've done a lot,

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:46.660
I think, to save the word Liberal.

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:48.610
I think I've really tried and
I've been one of the people

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.140
that have really tried to save it.

00:22:51.140 --> 00:22:53.220
So that a guy like you
or a guy like Ben Shapiro

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:55.427
could say, "Yeah, in the true
sense I'm a classical Liberal

00:22:55.427 --> 00:22:57.680
"and that really is modern Conservatism."

00:22:57.680 --> 00:22:59.750
So I would say more than
anything else, in a way,

00:22:59.750 --> 00:23:01.310
I'm a modern Conservative.

00:23:01.310 --> 00:23:04.990
If conservatism is what
I now believe it to be,

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.550
that it isn't that you have
to buy all of these ideas

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:10.230
but you can have some outlier ideas,

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:13.600
but basically believe in the
bedrock principles of America,

00:23:13.600 --> 00:23:16.140
then I would say I'm
a modern Conservative.

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:17.680
And I would just say
one other thing which is

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:19.610
that defending my Liberal principles

00:23:19.610 --> 00:23:22.010
has become a Conservative position.

00:23:22.010 --> 00:23:22.843
- Yeah, it's amazing.

00:23:22.843 --> 00:23:25.340
I always say that, too, I
always say I'm a Conservative

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.550
because I'm a Liberal.

00:23:26.550 --> 00:23:27.637
It's true.

00:23:27.637 --> 00:23:30.370
"Don't Burn This Book," it's terrific

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:32.590
and I'm glad to see that
more people are buying it

00:23:32.590 --> 00:23:33.423
than are burning it.

00:23:33.423 --> 00:23:35.114
Though I know there are a lot of liberals

00:23:35.114 --> 00:23:35.947
(laughing)

00:23:35.947 --> 00:23:37.720
who just wanna cross out
the first word of the title.

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:40.270
But it's always great to talk
to you, Dave, thanks a lot.

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:41.700
- Klaven, thanks for doing this.

00:23:41.700 --> 00:23:43.980
And you're back on the
show, I think, in June.

00:23:43.980 --> 00:23:45.770
We're doing it in person regardless

00:23:45.770 --> 00:23:47.080
of what Eric Garcetti says.

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:48.410
What do you say about that?
(Klaven laughing)

00:23:48.410 --> 00:23:51.643
- I will be there with my big
black mask on, you'll see me.

00:23:52.781 --> 00:23:54.610
- Thank you, my friend.

00:23:54.610 --> 00:23:56.000
- Thanks a lot, it's great
talking to you, Dave.

